<section id="S3.SS2.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.4 </span>Overall Pipeline.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.2.3 Projection-Based Sparse Coding ‣ 3.2 Dictionary-Based KV Cache Compression ‣ 3 Algorithm ‣ LLM Inference in a Flash!" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> illustrates our runtime pipeline for dictionary-based KV cache compression and attention during LLM decoding.
We maintain a two-part KV cache: (i) a long <em id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">compressed context</em> represented by sparse codes over a pretrained dictionary, and (ii) a recent <em id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">local window</em> stored in full precision to preserve fidelity for the most recent tokens.
During generation, newly generated KV entries are first appended to the local window (yellow blob).
When the window grows beyond its target size (we use a fixed local window size <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> in this work), we <em id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">batch-compress</em> the oldest tokens in the window (green blob) in fixed-size chunks of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> tokens (the <em id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">compression unit</em>), convert their keys and values into sparse codes using the pretrained dictionary, append these codes to the compressed context (dotted region), and evict the corresponding dense KV tensors from the raw cache.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the compressed context, we avoid explicit materialization of keys and values.
We first project the query onto the dictionary (<math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="QD^{\mathsf{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>𝖳</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">QD^{\mathsf{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>) and use the sparse codes to assemble approximate attention logits <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="QK^{\mathsf{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>𝖳</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">QK^{\mathsf{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In parallel, we compute exact attention over the dense local window.
We then compute the attention output by aggregating values directly from the value dictionary using the stored sparse coefficients, and combine contributions from the compressed context and the local window under a single softmax normalization.
To support this pipeline efficiently, the runtime stores only sparse codes for the compressed region and a bounded dense buffer for the local window.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.5 </span>Query-Aware Hierarchical Sparsity</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To further reduce compute and read I/O, we allocate reconstruction budget non-uniformly across tokens in a query-aware manner, inspired by prior work on query-aware sparsification and hierarchical attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>.
Our key idea is a two-stage refinement scheme: we first construct a coarse reconstruction for all tokens using a small budget <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\mathrm{low}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>low</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\mathrm{low}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and then selectively refine only the most relevant tokens up to a larger budget <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\mathrm{high}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>high</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\mathrm{high}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Concretely, we form a coarse key approximation for each token <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> using only its first <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\mathrm{low}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>low</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\mathrm{low}}</annotation></semantics></math> dictionary atoms and compute a proxy attention logit:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Score}(i)=Q\cdot\Big(\sum_{j=1}^{K_{\mathrm{low}}}\gamma_{i,j}\,\mathbf{d}_{\mathrm{idx}_{i,j}}\Big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Score</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>low</mi></msub></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐝</mi><msub><mi>idx</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Score}(i)=Q\cdot\Big(\sum_{j=1}^{K_{\mathrm{low}}}\gamma_{i,j}\,\mathbf{d}_{\mathrm{idx}_{i,j}}\Big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">We then select the top-<math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\%</annotation></semantics></math> tokens by <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Score}(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Score</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Score}(i)</annotation></semantics></math> and refine them by increasing the reconstruction budget from <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\mathrm{low}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>low</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\mathrm{low}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\mathrm{high}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>high</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\mathrm{high}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We compute attention under a single numerically stable softmax over this mixed representation (top tokens at <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\mathrm{high}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>high</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\mathrm{high}}</annotation></semantics></math>, others at <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\mathrm{low}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>low</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\mathrm{low}}</annotation></semantics></math>), concentrating dictionary reads and near-data compute on the most relevant tokens.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS6" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.6 </span>Why Flash CIM?</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our dictionary-based KV representation is well matched to flash-based compute-in-memory (CIM), but less attractive on conventional accelerators such as GPUs, as they do not support compute-in-memory.
The method requires repeated projections against a large dictionary (e.g., <math id="S3.SS2.SSS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="QD^{\mathsf{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>𝖳</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">QD^{\mathsf{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>) and gather-style aggregation from sparse codes.
On conventional accelerators, this often means repeatedly streaming the dictionary (and related intermediates) from HBM, increasing memory traffic and potentially dominating runtime.
Moreover, LLM decoding is frequently memory-bandwidth-bound even without compression; the additional dictionary reads and compute can offset the benefits of a more compact KV representation, yielding limited gains or slowdowns.
In contrast, flash CIM can keep the dictionary resident in flash and execute projection/gather near data, exploiting high internal read bandwidth while reducing off-chip transfers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">That said, dictionary-based KV coding can still be useful on conventional accelerators when the KV cache no longer fits in device memory (e.g., very long contexts or large batches), by storing sparse codes on the accelerator and reducing host–device offload bandwidth.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>System Modeling</h2>

<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Overview and metrics.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We implement an analytical Python model to compare a PCIe-attached inference NPU baseline against our CIM-SSD (<em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">LLM-in-a-Flash</em>).
The model targets absolute estimates grounded in published hardware data (see Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A System Modeling Parameters ‣ LLM Inference in a Flash!" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>) and is designed to capture relative trends with respect to context length, controller SRAM capacity, and pursuit placement.
We focus on decoding behavior and report latency (throughput) and total per-token energy, with a breakdown that highlights KV write and read costs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Systems and workload.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare four configurations spanning two NPU baselines and two CIM-SSD variants.
The first baseline <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">B1</span> is an inference NPU with DDR5 DRAM that stores both model weights and the full FP16 KV cache.
A second baseline <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">B2</span> uses the same NPU+DRAM hardware but applies our dictionary-based KV cache compression, storing dictionaries and compressed codes in DRAM and performing compression/reconstruction on the NPU.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The proposed CIM-SSD system <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S1</span> integrates an SSD controller (INT8/INT32) with on-controller SRAM, a CIM flash array that stores stationary weights and per-layer dictionaries, and DRAM as a backing store (Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.2.3 Projection-Based Sparse Coding ‣ 3.2 Dictionary-Based KV Cache Compression ‣ 3 Algorithm ‣ LLM Inference in a Flash!" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>(a)).
We assume PCIe Gen4<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>4 (8 GB/s) for the host–SSD link and a 16 GB/s internal bus between the controller and CIM array.
Additionally, we explore a second configuration, <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S2</span>, which accounts for limited on-controller SRAM capacity, placing compressed codes on DRAM and loading this to the CPU during reconstruction (while still applying pursuit on CIM).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">All configurations model Llama 3.1–8B with GQA (<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L{=}32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L{=}32</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{Q}{=}32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{Q}{=}32</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{KV}{=}8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{KV}{=}8</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d{=}128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d{=}128</annotation></semantics></math>), keeping <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> (query-side compute) separate from <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{KV}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{KV}</annotation></semantics></math> (stored KV state) so that operations scaling with query heads versus stored KV heads are modeled distinctly.
We focus on autoregressive decoding (batch size 1), where KV-cache access dominates.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Cost primitives and overlap.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We decompose each decode step into compute and data movement.
Each engine has peak throughput <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> (TOPS). For <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{op}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>op</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{op}}</annotation></semantics></math> operations,</p>
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\text{comp}}=\frac{N_{\text{op}}}{P},\qquad E_{\text{comp}}=N_{\text{op}}\cdot e_{\text{op}}+\left\lceil\frac{N_{\text{op}}}{S}\right\rceil\cdot e_{\text{ovhd}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>comp</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>op</mtext></msub><mi>P</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>comp</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>op</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mtext>op</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>⌈</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>op</mtext></msub><mi>S</mi></mfrac><mo rspace="0.055em">⌉</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mtext>ovhd</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\text{comp}}=\frac{N_{\text{op}}}{P},\qquad E_{\text{comp}}=N_{\text{op}}\cdot e_{\text{op}}+\left\lceil\frac{N_{\text{op}}}{S}\right\rceil\cdot e_{\text{ovhd}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is SIMD width and <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{\text{ovhd}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mtext>ovhd</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{\text{ovhd}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a per-SIMD-group instruction overhead.
Transfers follow a bandwidth model with optional fixed protocol terms for interconnects:</p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\text{xfer}}=t_{\ell}+t_{o}+\frac{\text{Bytes}}{B},\qquad E_{\text{xfer}}=8\cdot\text{Bytes}\cdot e_{b}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>xfer</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mtext>Bytes</mtext><mi>B</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>xfer</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext>Bytes</mtext><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\text{xfer}}=t_{\ell}+t_{o}+\frac{\text{Bytes}}{B},\qquad E_{\text{xfer}}=8\cdot\text{Bytes}\cdot e_{b},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> is the latency before the transfer starts (link latency), and <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{o}</annotation></semantics></math> is the fixed overhead associated with initiating the transfer, with <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{\ell},t_{o})\!&gt;\!0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.108em">&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{\ell},t_{o})\!&gt;\!0</annotation></semantics></math> for PCIe/internal buses and <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{\ell},t_{o})\!=\!0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{\ell},t_{o})\!=\!0</annotation></semantics></math> for DRAM/flash, and where <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is the bandwidth. Primitive energies follow Horowitz-style baselines <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> with 7 nm-class calibration guided by TPU-era references <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>; CIM periphery is treated as an older-node process.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Within a stage, we model overlap between independent compute and communication engines using</p>
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{overlap}(t_{1},t_{2})=\max(t_{1},t_{2})+(1-\rho)\min(t_{1},t_{2})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">overlap</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{overlap}(t_{1},t_{2})=\max(t_{1},t_{2})+(1-\rho)\min(t_{1},t_{2}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho{=}0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho{=}0.8</annotation></semantics></math> for the NPU baseline and <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho{=}0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho{=}0.9</annotation></semantics></math> for CIM-SSD, applied only when concurrency is plausible (e.g., CIM matmul with internal-bus transfers).
Across stages of a decode step (QKV projection <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> KV write <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> KV read/attention <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> FFN), execution is sequential due to shared engine/channel contention.
Pursuit iterations are also sequential (<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho{=}0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho{=}0</annotation></semantics></math>) due to <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}^{(i+1)}=\mathbf{r}^{(i)}-\alpha_{i}\mathbf{d}_{j_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐫</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐫</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐝</mi><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}^{(i+1)}=\mathbf{r}^{(i)}-\alpha_{i}\mathbf{d}_{j_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Layer modeling.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each decode layer follows a standard transformer decode pipeline (QKV projection, RoPE, KV-cache update, attention, output projection, residual paths, SwiGLU FFN, and RMSNorm). Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.2.3 Projection-Based Sparse Coding ‣ 3.2 Dictionary-Based KV Cache Compression ‣ 3 Algorithm ‣ LLM Inference in a Flash!" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>(b) illustrates the CIM-SSD dataflow.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Linear layers</span> (QKV, output, and FFN projections) are modeled as GEMV at batch size 1.
In the baseline, the NPU streams FP16 weights from DRAM every token, making these projections primarily memory-bandwidth bound.
In CIM-SSD (Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.2.3 Projection-Based Sparse Coding ‣ 3.2 Dictionary-Based KV Cache Compression ‣ 3 Algorithm ‣ LLM Inference in a Flash!" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>(b), <span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="15.74" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-3.41px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 15.74 15.74" width="15.74"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,15.74) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(7.87,0) translate(0,7.87)"><path style="fill:none" d="M 7.59 0 C 7.59 4.19 4.19 7.59 0 7.59 C -4.19 7.59 -7.59 4.19 -7.59 0 C -7.59 -4.19 -4.19 -7.59 0 -7.59 C 4.19 -7.59 7.59 -4.19 7.59 0 Z M 0 0"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -3.46 -4.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="6.92"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_text">1</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>–<span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.pic2" class="ltx_picture" height="15.74" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-3.41px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 15.74 15.74" width="15.74"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,15.74) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(7.87,0) translate(0,7.87)"><path style="fill:none" d="M 7.59 0 C 7.59 4.19 4.19 7.59 0 7.59 C -4.19 7.59 -7.59 4.19 -7.59 0 C -7.59 -4.19 -4.19 -7.59 0 -7.59 C 4.19 -7.59 7.59 -4.19 7.59 0 Z M 0 0"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -3.46 -4.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="6.92"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.pic2.1" class="ltx_text">3</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>), weights remain stationary in flash: the controller broadcasts the INT8 activation <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> over the internal bus, the CIM array performs in-place INT8 MAC, and INT32 partial sums return to the controller for requantization back to INT8.
CIM compute and internal-bus transfers are overlapped within the linear stage.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Nonlinear operations</span> (RMSNorm, softmax, SiLU, RoPE, residual add, and post-Swish multiply) run on the NPU in FP32 for the baseline.
On CIM-SSD, they execute on the SSD controller using integer-only approximations (INT8/INT32 + requantization), e.g., polynomial/shift-based <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>exp</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp</annotation></semantics></math> for IntSoftmax and integer sqrt/divide for IntRMSNorm, without relying on floating-point units.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">KV cache write path.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each new token appends its INT8 key and value vectors to a <em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">local window</em> in controller SRAM (Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.2.3 Projection-Based Sparse Coding ‣ 3.2 Dictionary-Based KV Cache Compression ‣ 3 Algorithm ‣ LLM Inference in a Flash!" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>(b), <span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="15.74" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-3.41px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 15.74 15.74" width="15.74"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,15.74) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(7.87,0) translate(0,7.87)"><path style="fill:none" d="M 7.59 0 C 7.59 4.19 4.19 7.59 0 7.59 C -4.19 7.59 -7.59 4.19 -7.59 0 C -7.59 -4.19 -4.19 -7.59 0 -7.59 C 4.19 -7.59 7.59 -4.19 7.59 0 Z M 0 0"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -3.46 -4.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="6.92"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.pic1.1" class="ltx_text">4</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>-a).
Periodically, the oldest entries are batch-compressed using pursuit (<span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.pic2" class="ltx_picture" height="15.74" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-3.41px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 15.74 15.74" width="15.74"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,15.74) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(7.87,0) translate(0,7.87)"><path style="fill:none" d="M 7.59 0 C 7.59 4.19 4.19 7.59 0 7.59 C -4.19 7.59 -7.59 4.19 -7.59 0 C -7.59 -4.19 -4.19 -7.59 0 -7.59 C 4.19 -7.59 7.59 -4.19 7.59 0 Z M 0 0"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -3.46 -4.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="6.92"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.pic2.1" class="ltx_text">4</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>-b): the controller sends the current residual <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}</annotation></semantics></math> (INT8) to the CIM array over the internal bus; the CIM array computes <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\top}\!\mathbf{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐫</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\top}\!\mathbf{r}</annotation></semantics></math> in-place and returns the selected atom and its corresponding index/coefficients; the controller then updates the residual in INT32 and records the sparse code.
After a fixed number of pursuit iterations, each token is represented as a compact set of (index, coefficient) pairs stored in SRAM, while all intermediate state (residual buffers and partial codes) remains on-controller throughout the loop. In the baseline (<span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">B1</span>), KV vectors are written in FP16 directly to DRAM.
In <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">B2</span>, the same compression runs on the NPU, but dictionaries and codes reside in DRAM, introducing additional DRAM transfers during encoding.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px6" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">KV cache read path.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The read path combines two regions, an uncompressed <em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">local window</em> and a <em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">compressed context</em>, with scenario-specific data movement (Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.2.3 Projection-Based Sparse Coding ‣ 3.2 Dictionary-Based KV Cache Compression ‣ 3 Algorithm ‣ LLM Inference in a Flash!" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>(b), <span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="15.74" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-3.41px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 15.74 15.74" width="15.74"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,15.74) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(7.87,0) translate(0,7.87)"><path style="fill:none" d="M 7.59 0 C 7.59 4.19 4.19 7.59 0 7.59 C -4.19 7.59 -7.59 4.19 -7.59 0 C -7.59 -4.19 -4.19 -7.59 0 -7.59 C 4.19 -7.59 7.59 -4.19 7.59 0 Z M 0 0"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -3.46 -4.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="6.92"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.pic1.1" class="ltx_text">5</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>–<span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.pic2" class="ltx_picture" height="15.74" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-3.41px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 15.74 15.74" width="15.74"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,15.74) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(7.87,0) translate(0,7.87)"><path style="fill:none" d="M 7.59 0 C 7.59 4.19 4.19 7.59 0 7.59 C -4.19 7.59 -7.59 4.19 -7.59 0 C -7.59 -4.19 -4.19 -7.59 0 -7.59 C 4.19 -7.59 7.59 -4.19 7.59 0 Z M 0 0"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -3.46 -4.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="6.92"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.pic2.1" class="ltx_text">6</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>). <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">B1</span> reads the full FP16 KV cache from DRAM and runs standard attention on the NPU (<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="QK^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">QK^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>, softmax, and <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Score}\cdot V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Score</mtext><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Score}\cdot V</annotation></semantics></math>). <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">B2</span> reads compressed codes <em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">and</em> per-layer dictionaries from DRAM, reconstructs KV on the NPU, and then runs standard attention. The repeated dictionary transfer becomes a fixed per-layer cost during decoding.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S1 (CIM-SSD)</span> avoids both full-KV transfer and dictionary loading. For the <em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">local window</em>, the controller reads INT8 KV from SRAM and computes attention locally. For the <em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">compressed context</em>, attention operates in the dictionary-score domain without materializing full KV: the CIM array computes <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="QD_{K}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">QD_{K}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>, the controller combines the resulting scores with sparse codes to form attention weights and code aggregates, and the CIM array applies <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{V}</annotation></semantics></math> to produce the value-side contribution. The local-window and compressed-context outputs are then combined.
<span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S2</span> considers the memory constraints of the local buffer on the SSD by storing codes in DRAM and fetching them to the CPU when performing reconstruction.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px7" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">SRAM budget and overflow.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Controller SRAM must hold (i) an uncompressed local window (recent INT8 KV), and (ii) transient pursuit state (residual buffers, partial outputs, and control metadata) across all layers.
For <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S1</span>, it also must store compressed KV history codes (growing with sequence length).
Dictionaries are <em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> stored in SRAM; they reside permanently in CIM flash.
The dominant term is the compressed-history codes, which scale linearly with context length.
In <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S1</span>, the SRAM budget is sized to keep both the local window and the history codes on-controller for typical sequence lengths.
With <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S2</span>, the coefficients are stored in DRAM.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Results</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate our approach on Llama-3.1-8B and Qwen-2.5-7B.
We first ablate each component of our integer-only quantization pipeline (Section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 Integer-Only LLM Inference ‣ 5 Results ‣ LLM Inference in a Flash!" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>) and dictionary-based KV cache compression (Section <a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3 Dictionary-Based KV Cache Compression ‣ 5 Results ‣ LLM Inference in a Flash!" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>), and then present combined downstream task results (Section <a href="#S5.SS4" title="5.4 End-to-End Results ‣ 5 Results ‣ LLM Inference in a Flash!" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>).
Finally, we report system-level latency and energy projections from our analytical model (Section <a href="#S5.SS5" title="5.5 System-Level Modeling ‣ 5 Results ‣ LLM Inference in a Flash!" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Experimental Setup.</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For integer-only quantization, we calibrate smoothing parameters and quantization scales using the Pile validation set following SmoothQuant  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite>, and use group size of 128 for group-wise quantization.
For dictionary training, we extract key and value activations from Pile calibration sequences, train a separate overcomplete dictionary (<math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M{=}32768" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>32768</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M{=}32768</annotation></semantics></math> atoms) per layer and per key/value, and apply per-tensor INT8 quantization to the learned dictionaries.
We use a local window of <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W{=}128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W{=}128</annotation></semantics></math> tokens, and when this is full we compress the oldest <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=32</annotation></semantics></math> tokens from the window.
We use a sparsity budget of <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\mathrm{high}}{=}16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>high</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\mathrm{high}}{=}16</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\mathrm{low}}{=}3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>low</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\mathrm{low}}{=}3</annotation></semantics></math> for the hierarchical policy.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Integer-Only LLM Inference</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate the impact of our integer-only quantization pipeline on language modeling quality, reporting perplexity on WikiText-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> for Llama-3.1-8B.
Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.2 Integer-Only LLM Inference ‣ 5 Results ‣ LLM Inference in a Flash!" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> ablates each design decision cumulatively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Starting from the FP16 baseline, naive per-tensor W8A8 quantization incurs substantial perplexity degradation due to activation outliers.
Adding SmoothQuant <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite> significantly recovers quality by redistributing quantization difficulty from activations to weights.
Group-wise quantization along the hidden dimension further improves accuracy by providing finer-grained scale factors for both weights and activations.
Replacing floating-point nonlinear operations (Softmax, RMSNorm, SiLU) with our integer polynomial approximations introduces a modest perplexity increase, as the second-order approximations trade off small numerical fidelity for full integer-domain execution.
This degradation is mitigated by applying INT16 requantization at Softmax and RMSNorm inputs.
Our final end-to-end integer-only pipeline achieves 7.5803 perplexity, within 0.0349 of the FP16 baseline, while eliminating all floating-point computation from the critical path.</p>
</div>
